.SH Sequence numbers
Each event carries a \f[CR]seq\f[R] field that increases monotonically
within a session.
This allows clients to:
.IP \(bu 2
Detect dropped or reordered events
.IP \(bu 2
Correlate pre/post pairs (they share sequential numbers)
.IP \(bu 2
Order events relative to command responses on CHAN_COMMAND
.SH Hook registration
The observability system registers hooks using Bash\(cqs command
execution hook infrastructure:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]register_pre_command_hook()\f[R] \(en Called before each command
executes
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]register_post_command_hook()\f[R] \(en Called after each command
completes
.PP
These are the same hook points used by the debugger.
Both systems coexist: the observe hooks emit events while the debug
hooks check breakpoints and step conditions.
.SH Initialization and cleanup
\f[CR]observe_init(fd, level)\f[R] initializes the observability state
for a session, setting the output file descriptor and initial observe
level.
\f[CR]observe_cleanup()\f[R] tears down the state.
.PP
\f[CR]observe_set_level()\f[R] and \f[CR]observe_get_level()\f[R]
control the current level at runtime.
.SH JSON escaping
The \f[CR]json_escape_for_observe()\f[R] function escapes command
strings for safe embedding in JSON event payloads.
This handles control characters, backslashes, quotes, and other
characters that would break JSON syntax.
.SH Per\-session isolation
Because each client session runs in a forked child process, observe
state (level, sequence counter, output fd) is per\-session.
One client\(cqs observe level does not affect other sessions.
.SH Relationship to the debugger
The observability system and debugger both use pre/post command hooks
but serve different purposes:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Observe\f[R]: Passive monitoring.
Events are pushed without pausing execution.
Useful for logging, profiling, and real\-time monitoring.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Debug\f[R]: Active control.
Execution can be paused at breakpoints for interactive inspection and
stepping.
.PP
Both can be active simultaneously in the same session.
